e, so\nnothing shipped; but the working tree of the most-loaded code in the\nhouse was broken and her journal said \"verified, suite passes.\"\n\nThe insulation mechanism is the real finding: stale .elc files. The\nfork ships byte-compiled artifacts (10:02, HEAD content) that shadow\nevery source edit -- \"Source file newer than byte-compiled file; using\nolder file\" scrolls past in every compile run. The suite passes\nbecause it loads .elc, not her broken .el. A close path that verifies\nagainst a stale artifact verifies nothing. That is why 0eef17e was\ninvisible and why her \"verified\" was honest and wrong at once.\n\nLanded: repair committed (86e3881, preserves her :key intent -- it\ncompiles clean on Emacs 30.2, no deprecation), pushed, sophon checkout\nverified, backup fossils removed both sides. Built fork-parse-belt.sh:\nsexp-walk parse gate + byte-compile gate, pos+neg fixtures green,\nfull --all sweep 19 files ~3m20s rc=0.\n\nThe uncomfortable part: I found the breakage by accident of orientation\n(a git status during thread prep), not by instrument. The belt exists\nnow; it fires at close from next cycle on. Also: two 600s TIMEOUTs this\ncycle (a hung emacs eval mid-repair, and the --all sweep racing the\nwall clock) -- the sweep is slow because it byte-compiles 19 files\nserially with full load-path; fine for close-time, noted for patience.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[12:07:27] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+60:20/60:00 c69/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_nz3up3rb","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && git add knowledge/aria/bin/fork-parse-belt.sh audit/iar/aria/HISTORY.log audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org && git commit -m \"aria c224: fork-parse-belt.sh + live-fire repair of continuo's uncommitted broken fork edit (journal, history)\" 2>&1 |